    <main class="g">
      <h1>How to switch audio output on Windows 11</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Speakers, headphones, a monitor or a soundbar: here are four ways to
        change the sound output device in Windows 11, from the built-in options
        to a one-click shortcut.
      </p>
      <h2>1. Quick Settings (fastest built-in way)</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd>, or click the sound and network
          icons on the right of the taskbar.
        </li>
        <li>Click the arrow next to the volume slider.</li>
        <li>Select your device from the list.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>2. Windows Settings</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <b>Start → Settings → System → Sound</b>.</li>
        <li>Under <b>Output</b>, select the device you want to use.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>You can also set the volume of each device here.</p>
      <h2>3. The classic Sound panel</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd>, type <code>mmsys.cpl</code> and
          press Enter.
        </li>
        <li>On the <b>Playback</b> tab, right-click the device you want.</li>
        <li>Choose <b>Set as Default Device</b>.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>4. One click with AudioSwitch</h2>
      <p>
        If you switch between the same two devices every day, for example
        speakers and headphones, the steps above get tedious.
        <a href="/">AudioSwitch</a> is a tiny, free tray app: pick your two
        devices once, then <b>left-click</b> the tray icon to switch. The icon
        shows which device is active.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/#download">Download AudioSwitch</a>
        </div>
        <p class="meta" style="margin-top: 8px">
          Windows x64 · Portable · No installer
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How do I switch between speakers and headphones in Windows 11?</h3>
      <p>
        Use Quick Settings (<kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd>) and choose the device
        in the sound output list, or use AudioSwitch to toggle between two
        devices with a single click.
      </p>
      <h3>How do I change the default audio output device in Windows 11?</h3>
      <p>
        Go to <b>Settings → System → Sound</b> and select a device under
        <b>Output</b>. Alternatively, open <code>mmsys.cpl</code> and use
        <b>Set as Default Device</b>.
      </p>
      <h3>Why does Windows not switch to my headphones automatically?</h3>
      <p>
        That depends on the device, the driver and whether the headphones were
        already connected. If Windows keeps the old output, pick the device
        manually using one of the methods above.
      </p>
      <h3>Is AudioSwitch safe?</h3>
      <p>
        AudioSwitch is free and open source. The executable is not code-signed,
        so Windows SmartScreen may show a warning on first run. You can verify
        the file with the SHA-256 checksum on the
        <a href="/#download">download section</a>, check the VirusTotal report,
        or build the app yourself from the
        <a href="https://github.com/Tolovaj0/Audio-Switch">source code</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>Does AudioSwitch work on Windows 10?</h3>
      <p>
        AudioSwitch is built for Windows 11 and will likely work on Windows 10
        as well, but Windows 10 already has a faster built-in output menu on the
        taskbar.
      </p>
      <h3>Do I need to install it?</h3>
      <p>
        No. It is a single portable executable with no installer and no .NET
        installation required.
      </p>
